# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.blend).
#
# WHAT THIS BOOT IS FOR. The contract carried no blend state at all, so every pipeline
# this library made was opaque -- create_blend_state with nothing but colormask RGBA --
# and CoreAnimation composites its layers with PREMULTIPLIED ALPHA. A label layer whose
# background is transparent black was therefore drawn as OPAQUE BLACK. That is what the
# last photographs of the home screen show: every icon name in a black box, the Search
# pill a black rectangle, the dock edged in dark red, the widgets flat white or black
# slabs. Nothing refused and nothing crashed; the arithmetic was simply missing.
#
# rwa_render_shape now carries MTLRenderPipelineColorAttachmentDescriptor's eight blend
# properties per attachment, mapped by table (air_metal.c) and refused by name where they
# cannot be served. THIS BOOT IS THE MEASUREMENT OF THAT, and most of it is not a command:
# it is the window server compositing its own home screen through the driver, which it
# does about ten times a second as soon as backboardd is up.
#
# THE TRIPWIRE, FIRST, because it is the one thing that would make everything below
# meaningless. struct rwa_render_shape grew by 260 bytes AT THE END (792 -> 1052) and
# struct rwa_render_info by 8 (108 -> 116), and userland/src/redwoodmetal.m holds a copy
# of both typed out by hand. THE DRIVER AND libredwoodair.dylib MUST COME FROM ONE
# SNAPSHOT. Both files now _Static_assert those sizes and offsets, so a one-sided EDIT
# fails to build -- but a one-sided INSTALL still cannot be caught at compile time, and it
# has happened before (logs/235006-run.log carried a fresh driver and an hour-old
# library). What it looks like here: the library reads color_blend_count from past the end
# of the driver's shorter struct and gets a garbage number, which the refusals turn into a
# sentence rather than a picture. So before reading anything else:
#
#   grep -a 'and Metal.s descriptor has eight' LOG     # must print NOTHING.
#                                                      # A line here with a huge count is
#                                                      # the two struct copies disagreeing
#   grep -a 'blend state for only' LOG                 # must print NOTHING, same reason
#
# (-a on every grep in this file: a boot log has NUL bytes in it.)
#
# WHAT THE WINDOW SERVER ACTUALLY ASKS FOR -- MEASURED, and not from this file's
# predictions. logs/080121-run.log is a boot with these fields in the driver, and every
# number below is counted out of it:
#
#   grep -a 'blend: attachment' LOG
#
# READ THE COUNTS DIFFERENTLY IN A NEW LOG. That boot printed one line per attachment per
# pipeline -- 240 of them, with THIRTEEN DISTINCT among them -- and this snapshot prints
# each distinct line once and then on powers of two of its own count, carrying the count
# in a following "... and that is the N'th pipeline asking for exactly that". The reason
# is the one 854389a paid for: the loader keeps the first 256 KB of a log and the last
# megabyte and drops the middle, and one boot of Resident Evil 4 would be thousands of
# pipelines. So in a new log the numbers come from the "N'th" lines, and
# `grep -ac 'blend: attachment'` counts CONFIGURATIONS, not pipelines.
#
#   backboardd (the window server, over its two lives in that boot) -- 226 lines:
#     50   attachment 0 format 80 blend 1 rgb 1/5 op 0 alpha 1/5 op 0 writeMask 0xf
#     48   attachment 0 format 80 blend 0 rgb 1/0 op 0 alpha 1/0 op 0 writeMask 0xf
#    106   attachment 1 format 80 blend 0 rgb 1/0 op 0 alpha 1/0 op 0 writeMask 0x0
#      6   attachment 0 format 80 blend 1 rgb 1/1 op 0 alpha 1/1 op 0 writeMask 0xf
#      5   attachment 0 format 80 blend 0 rgb 1/0 op 0 alpha 1/0 op 0 writeMask 0x0
#      3   attachment 1 format 80 blend 0 rgb 1/0 op 0 alpha 1/0 op 0 writeMask 0xf
#      2   attachment 1 format 80 blend 1 rgb 1/5 op 0 alpha 1/5 op 0 writeMask 0xf
#      2   attachment 0 format 43 (declined by the format table, not by blending)
#      4   format 115, blending off
#   SpringBoard -- 12 lines:
#      4   attachment 0 format 80 blend 1 rgb 4/1 op 0 alpha 4/1 op 0 writeMask 0xf
#      4   the same with blend 0, and 2 of One/One, and 2 of format 10
#
#   and 2 more from this port's own triangle application, which is 240 attachment lines
#   over 127 pipelines in the whole log, 64 of those attachments blending.
#
# The FIRST of those lines is the one that fixes the black boxes, and it is exactly what
# CoreAnimation's own code predicted (CA::OGL::MetalContext::create_pipeline_state at
# 0x188378090, blend mode 3 "sov" of the table at 0x18855c092):
#
#            format 80 = MTLPixelFormatBGRA8Unorm, the display's own
#            blend 1    = blendingEnabled YES
#            rgb 1/5    = source One, destination OneMinusSourceAlpha   <- premultiplied
#            op 0       = Add,  writeMask 0xf = All
#
# `rgb 4/5` instead of `rgb 1/5` would be STRAIGHT alpha, which is the one wrong answer
# that still looks blended: the labels would come out washed out and half transparent
# rather than boxed. It has never appeared in a log.
#
# THE SECOND LINE IS THE TRAP, and it is as common as the first: 48 pipelines against 50.
# Those layers blend INSIDE THEIR FRAGMENT SHADER and leave the descriptor untouched, so
# it arrives at Apple's own defaults -- which
# -[MTLRenderPipelineColorAttachmentDescriptorInternal init] (0x181a2e9a8) writes as the
# word 0x78001080. In Apple's own precompiled archive the proportion is the same: 504 of
# 1079 pipelines carry no blend state, 472 are source-over.
#
# NOTE THAT EVERY FIELD OF THAT LINE IS NON-ZERO EXCEPT `blend 0`: source factor One,
# writeMask 0xf, operation Add. If this driver had decided "some field is set, so this
# descriptor means to blend", it would have blended those 48 pipelines TWICE -- about half
# the home screen. `blendingEnabled` is the only field that says so, and the library reads
# nothing else of an entry whose blending is off.
#
# And the 106 lines for ATTACHMENT 1 are the other thing no reading of the enumerations
# would have predicted: the window server really does build multiple-attachment pipelines
# whose attachments ask for DIFFERENT things -- attachment 0 blending with every channel
# written, attachment 1 with writeMask 0x0 and blending off. That is what
# Gallium's independent_blend_enable is for, and with it clear rt[0] would govern them
# all and attachment 1 would be written. 5 pipelines in that boot came back
# `writes 0x000000f0`, which is attachment 0 writing nothing and attachment 1 writing
# RGBA, so the bit went on and nothing was refused.
#
#   grep -a "the first such pipeline's label" LOG
#
# NEW IN THIS SNAPSHOT -- logs/080121-run.log predates it and has none of these lines, so
# an empty grep means an old driver and not a quiet compositor. One label is printed per
# distinct blend configuration, which is the whole of what a label has to say: the
# compositor has over a hundred labels a boot and thirteen configurations.
#
# This is the shortcut that answers which of the two a pipeline is, without decoding
# anything: CoreAnimation builds the label out of the specification it compiled, and
# emits a `B<mnemonic>` segment if and only if the fixed-function blend mode is not its
# "none" value. So
#
#   com.apple.coreanimation.draw.PBGRABsovXm_...   <- blend state is in THIS descriptor
#   com.apple.coreanimation.draw.PBGRAXm_...       <- blends in its fragment shader
#
# and the mnemonic names the mode: sov, dot, add, mul, din, sap, sbs, scn, ldg.
#
#   grep -a 'blend: the library blends' LOG
#
# What the library made of it, and the second number is the one to read:
#
#   blend: the library blends attachments 0x1 and writes 0x0000000f
#
# The write masks are four GALLIUM bits per attachment -- R 0x1, G 0x2, B 0x4, A 0x8 --
# and MTLColorWriteMask's run the OTHER WAY, Red at 1 << 3 down to Alpha at 1 << 0. All
# (0xf) and None (0) are the same number either way, and so are green|blue and red|alpha,
# so for those four masks these two lines agreeing proves nothing. For any other mask
# they MUST DISAGREE: a writeMask of 0x8 (Red only) must come back as 0x1, and 0xe
# (Red|Green|Blue) as 0x7. A log where 0xe went in and 0xe came back is a log saying
# nothing ever reversed the nibble.
#
# AND THAT IS THE ONE THING THIS BOOT CANNOT MEASURE. Every writeMask in all 240 lines of
# logs/080121-run.log was 0x0 or 0xf, and both of those are palindromes under the
# reversal -- so the home screen says NOTHING about the bit order, and a driver with the
# nibble backwards would composite it correctly. The evidence for the reversal is
# elsewhere and is not a boot: Metal.framework's own MTLColorWriteMaskString
# (0x181b08754) tests bit 3 for "R" and bit 0 for "A", and air_metal_test --draw-blend
# asks for 0x8, 0x1, 0xe and 0x7 on the host, where four of its cases distinguish the
# cast from the mapping. CoreAnimation can send a single-bit mask -- `setWriteMask: 1`
# for blend modes 24 and 25, `setWriteMask: 8` in its multiple-output clusters -- so if
# `writeMask 0x8` or `writeMask 0x1` ever appears in a log, THAT line is the device's
# measurement of it and is worth keeping.
#
#   grep -a 'this render pipeline is refused' LOG
#
# Every refusal, by name -- and the printing of it is new in this snapshot too, because
# -fail: only ever put the sentence in an NSError that CoreAnimation discards. NONE of the
# nine blend refusals fired in logs/080121-run.log, and none should on the home screen:
# not one of the 1079 pipelines in Apple's own archive uses a factor this library
# declines. If one does fire, the sentence says which field and why -- the blend colour is encoder state and
# this contract has nowhere for it, Source1 needs a second fragment output the frontend
# has not got, SourceAlphaSaturated means two different things in the alpha channel, Min
# and Max with a factor other than One are two different equations, and a blend over an
# integer attachment is none.
#
#   grep -ac 'blend: attachment .* blend 1 ' LOG      # distinct blending configurations
#   grep -ac 'blend: attachment' LOG                  # distinct configurations in all;
#                                                     # thirteen in logs/080121-run.log,
#                                                     # where 240 lines were printed
#   grep -a "that is the .*'th pipeline" LOG          # and how many pipelines each
#                                                     # configuration stood for
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# AND THE ONE COMMAND, which is not about blending: it is the proven path re-measured
# through the grown struct.
#
# metal_drv_test --draw-all builds a pipeline per fragment function of Apple's GL renderer
# through Metal's own API and draws each one, and a freshly made
# MTLRenderPipelineDescriptor carries exactly the untouched blend state described above --
# blending off, factors One and Zero, writeMask All. So every one of these draws now goes
# through the new fields with Apple's defaults in them, and the pixels must be the pixels
# of the last boot and not one bit different. That is the device's answer to the struct
# growth: if the two copies of rwa_render_shape disagreed, the colour formats would be
# read from the wrong offsets and these draws would fail before any blending mattered.
#
#   grep -a 'drew .* pixel' LOG
#   grep -a 'step:' LOG | tail -3
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT THE HOME SCREEN SHOULD LOOK LIKE AFTERWARDS, because that photograph is the test:
#
#   - icon labels as WHITE TEXT ON THE WALLPAPER, with no box of any colour behind them
#   - the Search pill a translucent rounded capsule with the wallpaper showing through it,
#     not a black rectangle
#   - the dock a translucent panel with NO dark red border -- that border was the edge of
#     an opaque draw, not a colour anyone chose
#   - the widgets translucent over the wallpaper instead of flat white or black slabs
#   - the wallpaper itself and the icon artwork UNCHANGED: they were already opaque, so
#     nothing in this work should move them. If the icons change too, something other
#     than blending changed with them.
#
# Two ways it could come back still wrong, and they look different:
#
#   - everything a little too dark, deepening wherever two layers overlap: the destination
#     factor read as DestinationColor instead of OneMinusSourceAlpha, which is what a cast
#     between the two enumerations produces (air_metal.c has the arithmetic)
#   - labels and panels washed out and semi-transparent rather than boxed: premultiplied
#     read as straight alpha, source factor SourceAlpha instead of One
#
# Both of those are refused by the host check (air_metal_test --draw-blend, 25 cases, each
# priced against six ways of being wrong), so neither should reach a photograph. If one
# does, the host check is measuring something the device does not do.

/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --draw-all /System/Library/Extensions/AppleMetalGLRenderer.bundle/default.metallib
